SEB C++ core
============

This directory implements structure construction, symbolic scattering expressions,
numerical evaluation, and USD scene export. C++ applications normally include
SEB.hpp. The Python bindings in ../pyseb/bindings use the same core library.

World interface and implementation
----------------------------------

World.hpp              Declares the World class and its public API. The following
                       source files implement the same class; they do not define
                       separate World classes.
World.cpp              Adds and links subunits and structures, registers models,
                       describes worlds, and prints structural information.
WorldGraph.cpp         Graph queries, path searches, reference-point discovery,
                       and reference-name parsing and validation.
WorldSymbolic.cpp      Generates symbolic form factors, amplitudes, phase factors,
                       and size expressions, including recursive builders.
WorldNumerical.cpp     Parameter handling, numerical scattering evaluation, and
                       numerical size calculations through Guinier coefficients.

USD export
----------

USDExport.hpp          Public units, layout modes, and export options.
USDExport.cpp          World::ExportUSD orchestration: expands nested occurrences,
                       assembles scenes, places instances, and commits the file.
USDGeometry.cpp        Realizes geometry, samples references, and supplies vector,
                       quaternion, and rotation helpers.
USDLayout.cpp          Geometry proxies, clearance calculations, and scoring for
                       readable layouts.
USDWriter.cpp          Writes USD primitives, metadata, and relationships, and
                       constructs Debye-cloud envelope meshes.
USDExportInternal.hpp  Private types and helper declarations shared by these
                       source files. Excluded from installed public headers.

Core types and numerical support
--------------------------------

SEB.hpp                Main include for C++ applications.
Abstract_subunit.hpp   ABSSubUnit base class for subunits and structures.
Structure.hpp          Structure wrapper around a graph of connected objects.
Subunit.*              SubUnit base implementation: scattering terms, reference
                       points, numerical evaluation, and validation helpers.
SymbolInterface.*      Symbol creation, parameter naming, and expression output
                       through the symbolic backend interface.
Types.hpp              Shared type aliases and containers.
Constants.hpp          Core enums and constants.
Exceptions.hpp         SEBException and call-stack reporting.
SpecialFunctions.*     Numerical special functions using GSL where needed.
SpecialFunctionsWrapper.*
                       Expression wrappers for special functions.
NumericalIntegration.* Quadrature support for integral-defined subunits.
SphereAmplitude.hpp    Stable numerical solid-sphere amplitude near zero.
Geometry/              Shared geometric types.

Models and file formats
-----------------------

Subunits/              Analytic, integrated, callback-defined, Debye-cloud, and
                       atomic SAXS subunit implementations.
SubunitIO/             YAML model parsing, expression parsing, registration,
                       bundled-model catalogue, and reference validation.
StructureIO/           PDB parsing, atom parameter profiles, and builders for
                       coordinate clouds and atomic SAXS models.
WorldIO/               YAML world definitions, parsing, and serialization.

Most bundled analytic models are defined in ../models/*.pyseb.yaml rather than
in individual C++ classes. Their catalogue is generated during CMake
configuration. See ../docs/file-defined-subunits.qmd for the model format.

The symbolic backend implementations live outside this directory:
../seb-symbolic/ contains the common interface and portable backend;
../seb-symbolic-ginac/ contains the optional GiNaC backend.

Building and testing
--------------------

Run these commands from the repository root, not from this directory:

    make core      Build the portable C++ core.
    make ginac     Build with the optional GiNaC backend.
    make python    Build the Python extension with the portable backend.
    make tests     Build the GiNaC core and run the C++ tests.

CMakeLists.txt in the repository root is the source of truth for library sources
and dependencies. Add new implementation files there. Tests and their build
instructions are in ../Tests/README.md; Python tests are in
../Tests/python/. See ../README.md for installation and documentation links.
